<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>UAVCAP-15K</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Compared to detection datasets, captioning datasets contain richer semantic information and are commonly used in conjunction with detection datasets during open-vocabulary detection (OVD) model pretraining. Datasets with detailed image captions significantly enhance the training of OVD models<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>. To construct a large-scale UAV-based caption dataset, we selected suitable images from the UAVDE-2M dataset and leveraged LVLM to generate image captions by integrating existing annotations. Specifically, we convert the absolute coordinates of objects in images into relative positional descriptions (e.g., top-left corner, bottom-right corner) and integrate them as part of the prompt. Simultaneously, the LVLM is required to maximize the description of object types, object actions, precise object locations, texts, doublechecking relative positions between objects, etc<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>. Given the prevalence of small-sized objects in UAV perspective images, our prompts explicitly emphasize the detailed description of small-scale objects while deliberately minimizing the description of large-scale background elements. We used the Qwen2.5-VL<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite> as the LVLM to construct UAVCAP-15K. The prompt and sample of UAVCAP-15K is shown in Figure <a href="#S3.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 3.2 UAVCAP-15K ‣ 3 Datsets ‣ Cross-Modal Enhancement and Benchmark for UAV-based Open-Vocabulary Object Detection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F5" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.06011v1/UAVCAP15K.png" id="S3.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:685/310;" width="685" height="310" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F5.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 5</span>: </span><span id="S3.F5.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Prompt and a sample of UAVCAP-15K.<span id="S3.F5.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> The UAVCAP-15K dataset utilizes a format of long-form, detailed descriptions for its captions. Moreover, it employs prompting techniques to enforce a consistent structure, ensuring all captions describe the image content in a uniform manner.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Methods</h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Cross-Attention Gated Enhancement Module</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To effectively integrate natural language guidance with visual features, we propose a novel fusion module, termed CAGE. It enriches spatial image features with semantic context derived from textual descriptions. The module employs a multi-stage architecture combining: (1) a cross-modal attention mechanism, (2) a spatial context refinement block with an adaptive gate, (3) a global FiLM-based conditioning layer, and (4) a residual connection. It operates on an image feature map <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{img}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{img}</annotation></semantics></math> and text embeddings <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{text}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{text}</annotation></semantics></math>. The detail structure of CAGE is shown Figure <a href="#S4.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 4.1 Cross-Attention Gated Enhancement Module ‣ 4 Methods ‣ Cross-Modal Enhancement and Benchmark for UAV-based Open-Vocabulary Object Detection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The cornerstone of our module is a multi-head cross-attention mechanism that generates a text-aware context map. Visual features <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{img}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{img}</annotation></semantics></math> are linearly projected to form the query tensor <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>. Concurrently, the text embeddings <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{text}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{text}</annotation></semantics></math> are transformed into key (<math id="S4.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>) and value (<math id="S4.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>) tensors, using two distinct linear projections. Each spatial location in the image feature map (a query) attends to all text tokens (keys) to compute attention scores. These scores are then used to compute a weighted sum of the text values (<math id="S4.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>), yielding a context vector for each spatial location. The attention output, which forms a context map <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{ctx}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{ctx}</annotation></semantics></math>, is computed as:</p>
<table id="S4.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{ctx}=softmax\left(\frac{QK^{T}}{\sqrt{d_{k}}}\right)V" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><msqrt><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msqrt></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{ctx}=softmax\left(\frac{QK^{T}}{\sqrt{d_{k}}}\right)V</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is the dimension of each attention head.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The raw context map <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{ctx}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{ctx}</annotation></semantics></math> is first refined through a lightweight depthwise separable convolution block to enhance local spatial coherence. To prevent visual information from being overwhelmed, we introduce an gating mechanism. This gate, implemented as a small convolutional head, generates a spatial mask <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\text{occ}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>occ</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\text{occ}}</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{img}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{img}</annotation></semantics></math> to adaptively modulate the intensity of the refined context map (<math id="S4.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{\prime}_{ctx}\odot M_{occ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{\prime}_{ctx}\odot M_{occ}</annotation></semantics></math>). The gated context is then concatenated with the original image features <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{img}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{img}</annotation></semantics></math> and merged via a 1x1 convolution.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">As a parallel path for global conditioning, we employ Feature-wise Linear Modulation (FiLM). Global average-pooled text features are passed through an MLP to predict channel-wise scale (<math id="S4.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>) and bias (<math id="S4.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>) parameters. These parameters modulate the merged features:</p>
<table id="S4.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{out}=F_{merged}\odot(1+\gamma)+\beta" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{out}=F_{merged}\odot(1+\gamma)+\beta</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">Finally, a residual connection from the input <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{img}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{img}</annotation></semantics></math> is added to the modulated output to preserve the original visual information pathway, followed by a Batch Normalization layer to stabilize the final output.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F6" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.06011v1/CAGE.svg" id="S4.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:685/212;" width="685" height="212"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F6.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 6</span>: </span><span id="S4.F6.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Detail of cross-attention gated enhancement module.<span id="S4.F6.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> The A is the overview of cross-attention gated enhancement module, B and C are the detail of gated head and cross attention respectively.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Module Embedding</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To validate the efficacy and versatility of our proposed Cross-Attention Gated Enhancement (CAGE) module, we integrate it into the state-of-the-art real-time open-vocabulary detection framework, YOLO-World-v2. Our integration strategy is designed to be seamless, positioning the CAGE module as a extitdrop-in replacement for the original text-vision fusion blocks, thereby demonstrating its utility as a flexible, plug-and-play component for various OVD models.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Integration Strategy.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The core architecture of YOLO-World-v2 features a sophisticated neck structure, derived from the Path Aggregation Network (PAN), which is responsible for fusing multi-scale visual features. Within this neck, the T-CSPLayer (Text-guided Cross-Stage Partial Layer) serves as the primary component for injecting textual guidance into the visual processing pipeline. We identify these T-CSPLayers as the ideal integration points.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Our strategy involves replacing every instance of the T-CSPLayer in the YOLO-World-v2 neck with our CAGE module. This ensures that text-vision fusion is consistently handled by our proposed method across all feature scales (e.g., P3, P4, P5). The process is facilitated by a strict alignment of the input/output (I/O) dimensions:</p>
<ul id="S4.I1" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">extbfInput: For each CAGE module, the visual feature map <math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{img}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{img}</annotation></semantics></math> is taken directly from the corresponding level of the PAN. The text embeddings <math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{text}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{text}</annotation></semantics></math> are supplied by the model’s shared text encoder (e.g., CLIP text encoder).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">extbfOutput: Our CAGE module is carefully configured to produce an output tensor that has the exact same channel count and spatial resolution as the T-CSPLayer it replaces. This ensures that the data flow to subsequent layers in the YOLO-World-v2 neck and head remains undisturbed, requiring no further architectural modifications.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Rationale and Advantages.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This substitution is motivated by the advanced capabilities of our module. While the T-CSPLayer provides effective fusion, our CAGE module offers a more nuanced mechanism. The multi-head cross-attention facilitates a finer-grained alignment between specific visual regions and semantic concepts in the text. Furthermore, the inclusion of the extitocclusion gate and the global FiLM conditioning path provides a more robust fusion that preserves critical visual details while adaptively leveraging linguistic guidance. By implementing this as a drop-in replacement, we minimize architectural disruption, allowing us to fairly evaluate the direct performance impact of our fusion design. This modularity also highlights the potential for the CAGE module to be easily adapted to other vision-language architectures that require sophisticated cross-modal feature fusion.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Experiments</h2>

<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Implementation details</h3>

<section id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Data.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Following prior work<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>, we jointly pre-train our model on a combination of detection and captioning datasets, namely UAVDE-2M and UAVCAP-15K, where images from VisDrone<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite> are excluded.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Training.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All models we trained were processed for 100 epochs on a setup of 4<math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math>A800 GPUs. We set the batch-size as 128, for data augmentation, we adopted the default strategy and parameters provided by Ultralytics<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Evaluation.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">During the evaluation phase, we employ a text-prompting approach. For each dataset, we utilize its class names directly as the text prompts.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Comparison Results</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The zero-shot evaluation results of our proposed method on the VisDrone test set are presented in Table <a href="#S5.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 5.2 Comparison Results ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Cross-Modal Enhancement and Benchmark for UAV-based Open-Vocabulary Object Detection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. The analysis first highlights the efficacy of domain-specific pre-training. Compared to the official YOLO-World-v2 models pre-trained on a general-purpose dataset (OGC), the models trained on our custom UAVDE-2M and UAVCAP-15K datasets exhibit substantial performance improvements. For instance, the mAP of the YOLO-World-v2-L model significantly increases from 8.59 to 12.2, which strongly demonstrates that our datasets can effectively enhance the model’s zero-shot generalization capability in aerial imagery scenarios. Furthermore, our proposed method demonstrates consistent superiority over the baselines under identical training conditions. Our models, outperform the original YOLO-World-v2 across all scales (S/M/L), achieving mAP scores of 10.0, 12.5, and 13.9, respectively, which are notable improvements over the baseline scores of 9.6, 11.1, and 12.2. In addition to its accuracy advantages, our method also excels in model efficiency. Taking the L-scale model as an example, ours boosts the mAP from 12.2 to 13.9 while simultaneously reducing the parameter count from 48M to 34M and GFLOPs from 204.5 to 144.0. This result underscores the ability of our method to significantly reduce model complexity and computational cost without sacrificing performance, making it highly suitable for resource-constrained edge computing applications. In summary, the experimental results thoroughly validate the dual effectiveness of our proposed datasets and method, achieving concurrent improvements in both accuracy and efficiency for the task of zero-shot object detection in UAV-based images.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S5.T2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 2</span>: </span><span id="S5.T2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Zero-shot Evaluation on VisDrone.<span id="S5.T2.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> We evaluate out methods on VisDrone<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite> test set in a zero-shot manner. For models trained on standard benchmark datasets, we adopt the weights released by Ultralytics<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>. OGC indicates Objects365<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite>, GoldG<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite> and CC3M<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>. UAV indicates to UAVDE-2M and UAVCAP-15K.</span></span></figcaption>
<table id="S5.T2.6" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S5.T2.6.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T2.6.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">Methods</th>
<th id="S5.T2.6.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">Data</th>
<th id="S5.T2.6.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">Size</th>
<th id="S5.T2.6.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">Epochs</th>
<th id="S5.T2.6.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">Params(M)</th>
<th id="S5.T2.6.1.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">GFLOPs</th>
<th id="S5.T2.6.1.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">AP50</th>
<th id="S5.T2.6.1.8" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">mAP</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S5.T2.6.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T2.6.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">YOLO-World-S</td>
<td id="S5.T2.6.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">OGC</td>
<td id="S5.T2.6.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">640</td>
<td id="S5.T2.6.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">-</td>
<td id="S5.T2.6.2.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">13</td>
<td id="S5.T2.6.2.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">71.5</td>
<td id="S5.T2.6.2.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">7.44</td>
<td id="S5.T2.6.2.8" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">4.32</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T2.6.3" class="ltx_tr">
